															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/manymeats" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2104082); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=2104082&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1605795061" alt="ManyMeats" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=2104082)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/manymeats" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2104082); return false;" >ManyMeats</a></span> <span class="odate time_1583426946 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">05 Mar 2020 16:49</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4533099">
									

<p>This is a novel an interesting concept which is an often rare and precious thing. I think a lot of people are afraid to tackle social media / more modern societal concepts in their SCP's because of potential audience and tone mis-matching but this piece does a fairly good job of pursuing that.</p>
<p>I will say that your lampshading of the SCP's conduct is necessary and used fairly effectively, but there are stretches of the dialogue that are stilted and difficult to love.</p>
<p>As a personal preference, I don't think that the Foundation would dehumanize an anomaly by referring to it only as an SCP. Yes, I get the reason and historicity as to why, but it doesn't strike me as practical and personal <em>especially</em> when the interviewer is getting comfortable and saying &quot;Drop the Dr.&quot;</p>
<p>Having the interviewer be named Chameleon is striking me as odd but I am being told &quot;that's definitely a name people have&quot;. I'm not going to disagree with that assertion and I don't hold it against this piece.</p>
<p>Overall, this is novel and makes for a memorable character in spite of my hangups about the dialogue. I can comfortably +1 this.</p>

							</div>

								
					
